# Plan: Historical Shock-Period Stress Testing for walkforward_sim.py
Date: 2026-07-07
Status: READY FOR IMPLEMENTATION
Estimated complexity: S

## The Idea (one paragraph)
Live paper-trading over a few months is very unlikely to include a genuine
market shock, so strategies never get tested against the scenarios that
matter most for risk. This adds a `--stress` mode to `walkforward_sim.py`
that, instead of running once over `--start`/`--end`, replays the same
simulation independently over each of a small registry of known historical
shock windows (the Feb–Mar 2020 COVID crash, the 2022 selloff, plus room to
add more) and reports one set of metrics per shock period side by side with
the normal rolling-window run.

## Goal
One flag answers "how would this strategy have behaved during a real crash?"
without hand-picking date ranges every time.

## Design Decisions
- **Shock windows are a small, hardcoded, well-known registry**, not
  user-authored config in this version — they're historical facts, not
  opinions: `covid_2020` (2020-02-15 to 2020-04-15), `selloff_2022`
  (2022-01-01 to 2022-10-15), and `vol_spike_2018` (2018-10-01 to
  2018-12-24, the Q4 2018 selloff) as a third, less commonly cited one for
  variety. Extensible later via a `--shocks_json` override file, but the
  built-in three ship as the default and require no authoring to use.
- **Scope: `walkforward_sim.py`, both simulation paths**, matching the idea's
  named file exactly. `--stress` reruns the GBM `simulate_walkforward` path
  when a `--model` bundle is given, and is exposed as a reusable helper
  (`run_shock_tests`) that also accepts a `DailyStrategy` so the arena's
  generic path can call it too — arena CLI wiring (`arena-run backtest
  --stress`) is listed as a fast-follow issue rather than bundled here, to
  keep this plan's diff to one file's CLI.
- **Output**: one row per shock period (period name, window dates, Sharpe,
  CAGR, MaxDD, worst single day) printed as a table, plus one optional CSV
  per period if `--out_equity_csv` is given (suffixed with the period name).
  The normal `--start`/`--end` run, if also requested, prints first, so
  `--stress` is additive, not a replacement for the standard run.

## Out of Scope (this version)
- User-authored custom shock windows beyond the `--shocks_json` override
  hook (the hook exists; authoring UX is not polished this round)
- Wiring into `arena-run backtest` (fast-follow — the helper is written to
  be reusable, but CLI plumbing there is separate)
- Intraday shock analysis (`intraday_sim.py` / `PaperEngine`) — daily bars
  only, matching the rest of the arena's scope
- Statistical significance testing of shock-period results (small samples by
  construction; this is a diagnostic report, not a hypothesis test)

## Affected Areas
- New: `shocks.py` — `SHOCKS: Dict[str, Tuple[str, str]]` (name → (start,
  end) registry as above); `load_shocks(json_path: str | None) ->
  Dict[str, Tuple[str, str]]` (returns `SHOCKS` merged with/overridden by an
  optional JSON file of the same shape).
- `walkforward_sim.py` — `run_shock_tests(bundle_path_or_strategy, tickers,
  shocks, **sim_kwargs) -> pd.DataFrame` (one row per shock: runs the
  appropriate simulate function — `simulate_walkforward` if given a bundle
  path, `run_strategy_walkforward` if given a `DailyStrategy` instance —
  over each window, computes metrics via `analyze_equity.compute_metrics`
  plus a `worst_day` column, catches and reports per-window failures such as
  "no data available for this ticker in this window" without aborting the
  other windows). `main()` gains `--stress` (flag) and `--shocks_json`
  (optional override path).
- New tests: `tests/test_shocks.py` — registry shape, `load_shocks` merge
  behavior, `run_shock_tests` against synthetic multi-year data (mocked
  `fetch_history`, no live calls) producing one row per window with sane
  metrics, and a deliberately-missing-data window reporting a failure row
  instead of crashing the whole stress run.

## Implementation Order
1. `shocks.py` — registry + `load_shocks` + unit tests (dates, merge/override
   behavior, malformed JSON handling).
2. `run_shock_tests` in `walkforward_sim.py`, dispatching on bundle-path vs.
   `DailyStrategy` input; per-window try/except so one bad window doesn't
   kill the report; tests against synthetic data.
3. CLI wiring (`--stress`, `--shocks_json`) + printed table format
   (period, dates, Sharpe, CAGR, MaxDD, worst_day) + optional per-period CSVs.

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] `SHOCKS` contains at least `covid_2020`, `selloff_2022`, and
      `vol_spike_2018` with correct, well-known date ranges
- [ ] `load_shocks(None)` returns the built-in registry unchanged;
      `load_shocks(path)` merges/overrides by name from the JSON file
- [ ] `run_shock_tests` given a GBM bundle path produces one metrics row per
      shock window, each computed only from that window's data
- [ ] `run_shock_tests` given a `DailyStrategy` instance uses
      `run_strategy_walkforward` and produces the same row shape
- [ ] A shock window with no available data for any ticker produces a
      failure row (not a crash) that names the window and the reason
- [ ] `walkforward-sim --model ... --stress` prints the shock table in
      addition to (not instead of) the standard run when `--start`/`--end`
      are also given
- [ ] Tests pass: `uv run pytest tests/ -m "not exercise"` — no live API
      calls (fetch_history mocked with synthetic multi-year OHLCV)

## Edge Cases and Error Handling
| Situation | Expected behaviour |
|-----------|-------------------|
| Ticker has no history covering a shock window (e.g. IPO'd after 2022) | That ticker excluded from that window's universe; window still runs with remaining tickers; noted in output if the whole universe is empty |
| `--shocks_json` file malformed | Clear error naming the file and the parse problem; built-in registry NOT silently used as a fallback (avoids masking a typo) |
| `--stress` passed without `--model` and without any `--strategies` (generic path) | Clear `SystemExit` — nothing to stress-test |
| Overlapping or out-of-order shock windows in a custom JSON | Each processed independently; no assumption of chronological or non-overlapping order |
| A shock window entirely outside the fetched ticker's available history range | Reported as a failure row: "no bars in window", not an empty-but-silent success |

